@@ -0,0 +1,231 @@
+# Ghost preview network traffic
+
+Ghost previews show every player where each builder is about to place a block.
+In a 12-player test, ping rose to 300-400 ms once a few people were building,
+and other players' ghosts jumped around. This doc covers what the old design
+cost, what replaced it, and what players will notice.
+
+All numbers below are **estimates**, not measurements from this place. The
+per-message byte costs come from a desktop Studio measurement of Roblox remote
+and replication payloads (table below). The usage figures (frame rates, how
+often a cursor crosses into a new cell, how many people are building) are
+assumptions, stated wherever they are used. "How to verify" at the end gives
+the measurement that should confirm or correct these numbers.
+
+## Unit costs used
+
+| Item | Bytes |
+|---|---|
+| Remote call header, before any arguments | 9 |
+| `Vector3` argument | 13 |
+| Short string argument (about 2 + length; a 10-character string costs 12) | "Brick" ≈ 7 |
+| `nil` argument | ≈ 1 to 2 |
+| Replicated `CFrame` change on an anchored part | 25 |
+| Buffer argument overhead (a 16-byte buffer cost 28 bytes as a call: 9 + 3 + 16) | 3 + contents |
+
+These are payload bytes. Packet and UDP headers are left out of both the
+before and after figures, so the comparison holds.
+
+## Before
+
+### Design
+
+- `GhostPreview.client` fired `MoveGhost` (a reliable `RemoteEvent`) on
+  **every RenderStepped**, with `(blockTypeName, cellPosition)`, or
+  `(nil, nil)` when not building. Players who weren't building still sent
+  every frame.
+- The server moved a replicated `Part` per player in `Workspace.Ghosts`.
+  Writing the same value twice doesn't replicate, and several writes in one
+  server frame replicate as one. So each ghost sent one 25-byte `CFrame`
+  change to every client for each server frame (60 Hz) in which its cell
+  changed. Your own ghost also came back to you this way.
+
+### Client to server
+
+| Case | Per message | Rate | Per client |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Building, 60 FPS | 9 + 7 + 13 = 29 B | 60/s | 1.7 KB/s |
+| Building, 240 FPS | 29 B | 240/s | 7.0 KB/s |
+| Not building, 60 FPS | 9 + ≈2 = 11 B | 60/s | 0.7 KB/s |
+
+Server receives, 12 players:
+
+- **4 builders at 240 FPS, 8 idle at 60 FPS:** 4 × 7.0 + 8 × 0.7 = **33 KB/s**,
+  in 4 × 240 + 8 × 60 = **1,440 reliable remote calls a second**.
+- **Worst case, all 12 building at 240 FPS:** 12 × 7.0 = **84 KB/s**, in
+  **2,880 calls a second**.
+
+Two side effects came with this:
+
+- Client-to-server remotes are throttled at about 500 calls a second per
+  client, shared by every `RemoteEvent`. At 240 FPS the ghost alone used half
+  of that. On an uncapped client running above about 500 FPS, it used all of
+  it, and `PlaceBlock` calls got delayed behind it.
+- Everything travels on one ordered reliable stream. When a packet is lost,
+  all the ghost messages behind it wait for the resend.
+
+### Server to clients
+
+Assumption: a builder sweeping the cursor crosses into a new 4-stud cell on
+somewhere between a third of server frames (20 changes/s, "typical") and every
+server frame (60/s, "worst").
+
+- Per moving ghost: 20 to 60 × 25 B = **0.5 to 1.5 KB/s**, sent to every
+  client.
+- Per client, 4 builders: 4 × 0.5 to 1.5 = **2 to 6 KB/s**.
+- Per client, all 12 building: 12 × 1.5 = **18 KB/s** worst.
+- Server total (× 12 clients): **24 to 72 KB/s** with 4 builders, and
+  **216 KB/s** worst with all 12 building.
+
+Replicated property changes are reliable, in order, and unprioritized. The
+ghost `CFrame` stream shared that queue with everything else the server
+replicates. When the queue backs up, data ping rises for everyone, and ghost
+moves arrive in bursts. Clients drew each move the moment it arrived, so a
+burst looked like the ghost jumping.
+
+## After
+
+### Design
+
+1. **Each client draws every ghost itself.** The server has no ghost parts and
+   replicates no ghost properties. Ghost parts live in a client-only
+   `Workspace.GhostPreviews` folder, with `CanQuery = false` so placement
+   raycasts still pass through them.
+2. **Your own ghost is drawn locally every frame,** straight from your cursor,
+   with no network round trip.
+3. **Clients send only changes, at a capped rate.** `MoveGhost` is now an
+   `UnreliableRemoteEvent`. A client sends when its cell or block type changes,
+   at most once per 40 ms (25 Hz), whatever its frame rate. The rate is a
+   little above the server's 20 Hz batch rate so a steadily moving cursor
+   lands in every batch even though the two clocks are out of phase. At 60 FPS
+   that works out to every third frame, so 20 Hz in practice. An unchanged state
+   is resent once a second so the server recovers from a dropped message. The
+   payload is a 9-byte buffer (u16 sequence, u8 block index with 0 meaning
+   hidden, three i16 cell coordinates). The server ignores out-of-order
+   messages using the sequence number.
+4. **The server batches.** It keeps only each player's latest state. Every
+   50 ms it sends one `GhostBatch` (`UnreliableRemoteEvent`) to all clients,
+   holding every state that changed: an f64 server timestamp, then 9 bytes per
+   ghost (u16 slot, block index, cell). Every second it sends all states, which
+   repairs any batch that was dropped and fills in players who just joined.
+   A client that floods `MoveGhost` cannot raise what the server sends, since
+   each player gets at most one entry per batch.
+5. **Remote ghosts are interpolated.** Each client measures how long batches
+   take to arrive (server timestamp to `Workspace:GetServerTimeNow()` on
+   arrival, smoothed). It draws other players' ghosts that long plus 100 ms in
+   the past, gliding between the timestamped batch states. Because the times
+   come from the server, uneven packet arrival does not show up as uneven
+   motion, and the delay adapts to each client's ping.
+6. Each player's slot number reaches clients as the `GhostSlot` attribute on
+   their `Player`. Clients use it to skip their own entry, to ignore entries
+   for players who have already left, and to remove a ghost when its player
+   leaves.
+
+The wire format lives in `src/ReplicatedStorage/Shared/GhostCodec.luau`.
+
+### Client to server
+
+Per message: 9 header + 3 buffer overhead + 9 contents = **21 B**.
+
+| Case | Rate | Per client |
+|---|---|---|
+| Building, cursor moving, 60 FPS | 20/s | 0.42 KB/s |
+| Building, cursor moving, 120 FPS or more | ≤ 25/s | ≤ 0.53 KB/s |
+| Cursor still, or not building | 1/s | 0.02 KB/s |
+
+Server receives, 12 players (using the 25/s ceiling):
+
+- **4 builders, 8 idle:** 4 × 0.53 + 8 × 0.02 = **2.3 KB/s**, in at most
+  4 × 25 + 8 = **108 calls a second**.
+- **Worst case, all 12 building:** 12 × 0.53 = **6.3 KB/s**, in **300 calls a
+  second**.
+
+### Server to clients
+
+Per batch: 9 header + 3 buffer overhead + 8 timestamp + 9 per ghost =
+20 + 9N bytes. Batches go out only when a state changed, at most 20 a second.
+
+- **4 builders moving:** (20 + 36) × 20 = **1.1 KB/s per client**.
+- **All 12 moving, worst case:** (20 + 108) × 20 = **2.6 KB/s per client**.
+- Full resend: (20 + 9 × 12) × 1 = **0.13 KB/s per client** with 12 players.
+- Server total (× 12 clients): **15 KB/s** with 4 builders, and **32 KB/s**
+  worst with all 12 building.
+
+The 20 Hz cap makes these numbers independent of how fast anyone's cursor
+moves, unlike the old 20 to 60 changes a second.
+
+### Before and after, 12 players
+
+| | Before, 4 builders | After, 4 builders | Before, all 12 (worst) | After, all 12 (worst) |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| Server receives | 33 KB/s, 1,440 calls/s | 2.3 KB/s, ≤ 108 calls/s | 84 KB/s, 2,880 calls/s | 6.3 KB/s, ≤ 300 calls/s |
+| Each client receives | 2 to 6 KB/s | 1.1 KB/s (+0.13) | 18 KB/s | 2.6 KB/s (+0.13) |
+| Server sends, total | 24 to 72 KB/s | 15 KB/s | 216 KB/s | 32 KB/s |
+| Ghost traffic on the reliable stream | all of it | none | all of it | none |
+
+That is roughly **13x to 14x less** going into the server and about **5x to 7x
+less** coming out, and none of it is left on the reliable stream that
+`PlaceBlock` and other replication share. Where the ghost traffic grows with
+player count N, the costs are:
+
+- Server receives O(N) at a fixed per-player cap.
+- Each client receives O(N) at 9 bytes per moving ghost per batch.
+- Server sends O(N²).
+
+At 9 bytes a ghost, 30 players all building comes to about
+(20 + 270) × 20 ≈ 5.8 KB/s per client. That is still well under the roughly
+50 KB/s per client where developers report replication starting to queue.
+
+The biggest saving comes from the rate change (change-only, 20 Hz, no idle
+traffic) and from no longer replicating a part. The buffer packing is a
+smaller saving on top: the same data as plain arguments would cost about 40
+bytes up and 49 bytes per entry down.
+
+## What players will notice
+
+- **Your own ghost is more responsive.** It used to come back from the server,
+  so it trailed your cursor by your full ping (300-400 ms in the test). It now
+  moves on the same frame as your cursor.
+- **Other players' ghosts glide instead of snapping.** During a fast sweep a
+  remote ghost slides smoothly and can briefly sit between grid cells. It
+  always comes to rest exactly on the cell the builder is pointing at, and the
+  block they place doesn't change.
+- **Other players' ghosts trail a little more under good network conditions.**
+  On top of the network delay, they now trail by up to 40 ms waiting for that
+  client's next send, up to 50 ms waiting for the next batch, and a fixed
+  100 ms interpolation buffer. That adds roughly 100-190 ms over the old
+  design on an idle server. On
+  a busy server the old design was already 300-400 ms behind and bursty, so in
+  the conditions that prompted this change it should look better overall.
+- **A block type switch shows up on others' screens in the same delay window,**
+  and the color changes when the glide reaches the new state.
+- **Players who just joined may see ghosts appear up to a second late,** until
+  the next full batch. A ghost that is moving shows up as soon as it next
+  moves.
+- **A lost packet can leave someone else's ghost on a stale cell for up to
+  about a second,** until the next resend fixes it. Unreliable packets are
+  rarely lost, so this should rarely be visible.
+- **`Workspace.Ghosts` no longer exists** on the server or on clients. Nothing
+  else in this repo used it.
+
+Placement is unchanged: `BlockPlacement.server.luau` and `BlockPlacer.luau`
+were not modified, and placement does not depend on ghosts.
+
+## How to verify
+
+None of this has been run yet. To confirm these estimates:
+
+1. Start a local server with 8 to 12 players in Studio (Test, Clients and
+   Servers), or run another multi-player test session.
+2. Have 4 players sweep their cursors across the baseplate in build mode for
+   60 seconds while the rest stand still. Run it once on the old commit and
+   once on the new one.
+3. Record `Stats.DataReceiveKbps` and `Stats.DataSendKbps` on the server and on
+   one idle client. Record data ping from the Performance Stats bar, along
+   with network ping.
+4. Confirm by eye that remote ghosts glide, stop on the right cell, and hide
+   when a builder points at the sky. A placed block must land where the
+   placer's own ghost showed.
+
+Expect server receive to drop from tens of KB/s to a few, and data ping to
+stay close to network ping.
